  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The officer did not exercise effective control over the ABiH troops that committed the murders, and therefore cannot be held liable as a superior under Article 7(3) of the ICTY Statute.",
      "reasoning": "Effective control requires that the alleged superior have the material power to prevent or punish the commission of the crime. The facts show that the officer’s role was limited to coordination and monitoring, he was not the commander of the 9th Motorised Brigade, and the axis commander who issued the combat order was not subordinate to him. Consequently, the officer lacked the authority to issue commands to the perpetrators or to enforce discipline over them, which is a prerequisite for superior responsibility. In the absence of any evidence that he directed, ordered, or otherwise exercised command over the units that carried out the killings, the element of effective control is not satisfied.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The officer’s knowledge of the crimes arose only after the murders had been completed, and his subsequent instruction to cooperate with the investigation does not constitute a failure to punish that would trigger liability under Article 7(3).",
      "reasoning": "Article 7(3) imposes liability when a superior knows or has reason to know of the commission of a crime by subordinates and fails to take necessary and reasonable measures to prevent or punish it. The officer learned of the killings on the evening of 9 September, after the last proven murder, and immediately ordered cooperation with the civilian and military security services. This prompt action demonstrates that he did not willfully neglect his duty to punish; rather, he took steps to facilitate the investigation. Because the knowledge was contemporaneous with the crimes and he acted to assist the inquiry, the requisite failure to punish is not established.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The officer’s position as a rotating Deputy Commander and head of an Inspection Team does not meet the statutory definition of a “superior” capable of bearing command responsibility under Article 7(3).",
      "reasoning": "Jurisprudence distinguishes between de jure superiors (those holding formal command positions) and de facto superiors (those who exercise actual control). The officer’s appointment as Chief of the Main Staff and later as leader of an Inspection Team conferred limited coordinating functions and required consultation with the Commander on “drastic proposals.” He lacked the authority to issue operational orders to combat units and was not in the chain of command for the 9th Brigade. Therefore, he does not satisfy the legal threshold of a superior who can be held responsible for the acts of subordinates.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The evidentiary record does not establish the officer’s criminal responsibility beyond reasonable doubt, as required for conviction under international criminal law.",
      "reasoning": "Criminal liability, including superior responsibility, demands proof beyond reasonable doubt of each element: the existence of a superior-subordinate relationship, the officer’s knowledge of the crimes, and his failure to prevent or punish. The factual matrix provides only circumstantial indications of the officer’s involvement, without direct evidence that he ordered, planned, or facilitated the murders, nor that he had effective control over the perpetrators. Given the gaps in the factual foundation, the standard of proof is not met, and a conviction would be legally untenable.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}
